    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Cas9/AAV6 gene editing technique has been utilized to correct mutations in genetic diseases and is now being transferred to the clinic, undergoing clinical trials. In this technique, electroporation is used to introduce the components of the Cas9/AAV6 machinery into the cells being treated: complexed Cas9 protein and guide RNA (RNP), and adeno-associated virus (AAV). This treatment may be contributing to the observed cytotoxicity, eventually leading to cell death, in the treatment of hematopoietic stem cells. It is unknown exactly which component or combination of components is the largest contributor to the cells’ DNA damage response that leads to the cell deciding whether to temporarily enter cell cycle arrest and repair itself or to undergo apoptosis. Given the single-stranded nature of AAV6 (ssAAV6), we hypothesize that the cell mistakenly interprets this gene-editing component as a damaged piece of DNA, triggering the DNA damage response in a treated cell. Following the treatment of cells that separates the individual components of the Cas9/AAV6 machinery, a series of assays, measuring the amount of DNA damage in the cells and the percentage of the treated cell population that decides to opt between repair or death, demonstrate which component is the greatest contributor to cytotoxicity. As genome engineering techniques are used more frequently to treat patients, it is imperative that the efficiency and effectiveness of the techniques are maximized and that cytotoxicity of the treatment is brought to a minimum.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
